{"name":"Taiwan Blue Magpie","hex":"#3377a2","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"magpie","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of several kinds of bird in the family Corvidae, especially Pica pica.","A superficially similar Australian bird, Gymnorhina tibicen, in the family Artamidae."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To mark with patches of black and white or light and dark.","To steal or hoard (items) as magpies are believed to do."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/magpie"}],"entries":[{"title":"Taiwan blue magpie","blocks":[{"text":"The Taiwan blue magpie, also called the Taiwan magpie, Formosan blue magpie, or the \"long-tailed mountain lady\", is a bird species in the crow family. It is endemic to Taiwan."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Taiwan_blue_magpie_%28Urocissa_caerulea%29_Xindian.jpg/330px-Taiwan_blue_magpie_%28Urocissa_caerulea%29_Xindian.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taiwan_blue_magpie","wikidata":"Q94533"},{"title":"Red-billed blue magpie","blocks":[{"text":"The red-billed blue magpie is a species of bird (magpie) in the crow family, Corvidae. It is about the same size as the Eurasian magpie, but has a much longer tail, one of the longest of any corvid. It is 65–68 cm (25.5–27 in) long and weighs 196–232 g (6.9–8.2 oz)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Urocissa_erythrorhyncha.jpg/330px-Urocissa_erythrorhyncha.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red-billed_blue_magpie","wikidata":"Q766306"},{"title":"Urocissa","blocks":[{"text":"Urocissa is a genus of birds in the Corvidae, a family that contains the crows, jays, and magpies."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Urocissa_caerulea.jpg/330px-Urocissa_caerulea.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urocissa","wikidata":"Q904303"},{"title":"Taiwan","blocks":[{"text":"Taiwan, officially the Republic of China (ROC), is a country in East Asia. The main island of Taiwan, formerly known as Formosa, lies between the East and South China Seas in the northwestern Pacific Ocean, with the People's Republic of China (PRC) to the northwest, Japan to the northeast, and the Philippines to the south. It has an area of 35,808 square kilometers, with mountain ranges dominating the eastern two-thirds and plains in the western third, where its highly urbanized population is concentrated. The combined territories under ROC control consist of 168 islands in total covering 36,193 square kilometers. The largest metropolitan area is formed by Taipei, New Taipei City, and Keelung. With around 23.9 million inhabitants, Taiwan is among the most densely populated countries."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Flag_of_the_Republic_of_China.svg/330px-Flag_of_the_Republic_of_China.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taiwan","wikidata":"Q865"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/%D0%A2%D0%BE%D0%BB%D1%81%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%BA%D0%BB%D1%8E%D0%B2%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B7%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D1%81%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%BA%D0%B0.jpg/500px-%D0%A2%D0%BE%D0%BB%D1%81%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%BA%D0%BB%D1%8E%D0%B2%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D